<CHANGES>
###2026.08.23###
**The dashboard tile could freeze the whole webGui.** It read the controller
synchronously while Unraid rendered its own Dashboard page, so once a minute --
when the reader's 60-second cache expired -- a PHP worker was held for as long
as the card took to answer and everything else queued behind it. Reported as
~10 seconds of an unresponsive server, once a minute, on every install that had
the tile. It reads through the plugin's existing non-blocking cache now and can
never wait on hardware again; a test fails if that call is ever put back.

**Added**
- **Temperature unit** (Settings): °C or °F, default °C. Contributed by
  [Josh Mans](https://github.com/joshmans) as PR #22. Every sensor, stored
  value and threshold comparison stays in Celsius -- only the printed number
  changes.
- **Sortable tables.** Every column header on Drives, SMART, PHY and the Event
  Log sorts, ascending then descending, by click or keyboard. Capacities sort
  by real size, so 9.095 TB comes before 12.733 TB.
- **Unassigned drives are named.** A drive mounted by the Unassigned Devices
  plugin shows its mount label (`media1`, `media9`) in the Unraid column and on
  the Array Map, instead of an em dash. On a box with no array that column was
  previously empty on every row.
- **One HBA is one dashboard tile.** A dual-IOC board (SAS9300-16i and
  similar) reported as two tiles; it now shows as one, with a section per
  controller carrying its own temperature, address, drive count and health --
  matching what the Overview tab has always shown.

**Fixed**
- Drives whose serial contains a space -- several HGST SAS models do -- showed
  no `/dev` name on the Drives tab and no name in the Array Map tray, while
  SMART named them correctly. Four of nine drives on the reporting box.
- Long session URLs on the Settings page ran out through the right edge of the
  Export/API card.
- The dashboard tile showed one controller's PCI address as though it belonged
  to the board.

**Accessibility**
The plugin had no visible focus ring anywhere, and two inputs actively removed
the browser's. Bay cells and tray chips were plain `div`s with click handlers,
so the Array Map could not be operated by keyboard at all -- and emptying a bay
was double-click only, with no keyboard equivalent. The tab strip announced
itself to a screen reader as ten unrelated buttons.

- Focus rings on everything the keyboard can reach.
- The Array Map is fully keyboard-operable: Enter picks up and places a drive,
  Delete empties a bay, and focus stays where you were after the grid redraws.
- The tab strip is a proper tab list with arrow-key movement.
- Small controls meet the 24px pointer-target minimum.
- The two warning/gear glyphs are now SVG. As text they took *emoji*
  presentation on Windows and Android, which meant the warning sign beside the
  firmware flasher was rendered in the font's own colour and could not be made
  to look like a warning.

**Internal**
Design rules the CSS already followed are written down in
`design-system/MASTER.md`, and the colour tokens, the button, and the icon
sprite each have one home instead of two or three copies that had already
drifted apart. `docs/foreground-reads.md` records which code may block a
request, which is the rule whose absence caused the freeze above.

###2026.08.17###
SAS4 / 9600-series (`mpi3mr`) cards are now read instead of being named and
refused. Ported from techanonymous's `Unraid-HBAviewer-sas4` fork (MIT,
commit 882f88c) — the backend, the parsers, the Settings-page install helper
and the StorCLI2 installer script are their work, adapted into this repo's
existing structure and tests.

**Important:** no 9600-series card has run this port on this repo's own
hardware. techanonymous verified their original code on their own 9600-24i;
that is a real and useful data point, but it is not the same claim as this
repo having tested it. Treat this release as beta for SAS4 owners and report
back on [issue #19](https://github.com/FugginOld/Unraid-HBAviewer/issues/19).

**Added**
- Every tab except the firmware Event Log works against the Lite StorCLI2
  that the dkaser/unraid-storcli plugin ships.
- The Event Log needs Broadcom's FULL StorCLI2, which is proprietary and not
  downloadable automatically (its page is Cloudflare-gated with no stable
  URL). `scripts/install_storcli2.sh` does everything after you fetch that
  archive yourself: unpack, install to `/opt` (RAM on Unraid), and restore it
  at boot since the flash is FAT32 and cannot keep the execute bit. Backed
  up, idempotent, and driven from Settings → HBA Connection → Firmware Event
  Log.

###2026.08.16###
Every HBA in the machine is now monitored, not just one. Thanks to @brianara3
and @masterwishx, who reported the problem and then ran every test build on
their own hardware until it was right. Firmware/BIOS flashing remains off.

**Fixed**
- **A machine with more than one SAS2 card monitored only one of them.** The
  Overview, HBA Health, PHY Health, Event Log and Drives tabs all read the
  single card named by the "lsiutil Port" setting, while Detected Hardware
  listed them all — so the plugin looked complete and was watching one card.
  Every card is now read, each with its own temperature, firmware, PCIe link,
  drive count and PHY counters. Verified on a three-card box and a two-card
  box before release.
- **The Event Log was empty on every SAS2 card.** The log was being read
  correctly and then discarded, because the plugin expected a layout lsiutil
  does not print. One reporter's card held 85 entries behind that empty tab.
- **A card whose name contains a space showed only the first word.** A 9400
  read as "HBA" instead of "HBA 9400-16i", which also cost it its firmware
  check, since no name like that can be matched.
- **The two backends printed PCI addresses differently** — the same card read
  as "00:c1:00:00" or "c1:00" depending on which tool could see it. Both now
  print the short form that lspci starts with.
- **The diagnostic bundle collected only one card's telemetry**, which is why
  the bug above took three rounds of hand-written commands to pin down. It now
  captures every card.

**Changed**
- **The PHY tab's error counts now read zero after you reset the baseline.**
  The big number is what has happened since you last pressed the button, and a
  link you have fixed stops being flagged, instead of staying flagged until the
  next reboot. The driver's own running total keeps a line underneath, labelled
  "since driver load" — because that is what it is: it returns to zero when the
  driver reloads, with no cable having changed.
- **A dual-controller board renders as one card**, with a section per
  controller, rather than as two unrelated cards that happen to share a slot.
- The "lsiutil Port" setting no longer chooses which card is watched, since
  they all are. It stays as the fallback for a card table the plugin cannot
  read, and says so.

###2026.08.09###
Firmware/BIOS flashing is switched off in this release while it gets more
testing. Nothing else changes.

**Changed**
- **Firmware/BIOS flashing is disabled for now.** It is the one part of this
  plugin that writes to your controller, and a wrong or mismatched image
  bricks it permanently — so while it is being tested on more hardware it
  stays off rather than staying available and unverified. The Settings card
  is greyed out and the firmware page explains itself instead of offering
  the wizard. If you had flashing enabled, that setting is remembered and
  comes back with it; you do not need to do anything.
- Every request to the flashing endpoint is refused outright, not merely
  hidden — a page left open in another tab cannot reach it either. Monitor,
  Array Map, Locate, SMART, PHY Health, notifications and the diagnostic
  bundle are all unaffected.

###2026.08.08.1###
A card that cannot measure its own temperature now says so instead of
reporting zero. Thanks to @NotAnotherForumName, whose diagnostics bundle
showed a SAS9211-8i being asked for a reading it has no way to take.

**Fixed**
- SAS9211-8i and other SAS2008 cards showed a temperature of 0 °C in a
  "normal" band. Those cards have no onboard temperature sensor — but they
  answer the query anyway and return zero, which was read as a real
  measurement. That is worse than showing nothing, because a number in a
  green band looks like a live reading. They now show "N/A — no sensor". The
  card still counts as read, no temperature alert can fire from it, and
  cards that do have a sensor are unaffected. (#17)

**Changed**
- The Monitor and firmware pages load their behaviour from separate files
  now, rather than carrying it inline. Nothing changes on screen. The point
  is that this is the largest body of code in the plugin and, until now, not
  one of the tools that check this project for mistakes could open any of it.

###2026.08.08###
If you have drives sharing a controller port, your Array Map placements were
being overwritten by each other. Thanks to @PaliKinG3, who diagnosed the cause
correctly in the report and suggested the fix that was used, and to @jac2424,
whose SAS9207-8i turned up a second bug on the same page.

**Fixed**
- Assigning one drive to a bay placed every other drive on the same
  controller port into that same bay. The map keyed a placement on the
  CONTROLLER's Connected Port Number, which is not the drive's position and
  is shared — a SAS9305-16i reports 0(path0) for most of its drives, so one
  assignment captured all of them and emptied the Unassigned list. It now
  keys on the drive's own enclosure/slot, which is the backplane position and
  matches the label on the chassis. Nothing was ever lost: the map held a
  single entry the whole time. Existing maps are carried over automatically
  the first time you open the Array Map — a port that belonged to exactly one
  drive keeps its bay silently, and drives whose port collided return to the
  tray to be placed once. A drive that simply isn't present is never dropped.
  (#15)
- Bay cards on SAS2 cards driven by lsiutil (9207-8i and similar) showed no
  model, serial, temperature, health or capacity. That backend reports no
  serial at all, and the SMART cache was joined by serial only, so the join
  missed every drive on the card. It falls back to /dev now, and the
  collector caches each drive's capacity. Values the controller reports
  itself still win. (#15)
- Host Link reported no PCIe maximum on SAS2 cards, so a downtrained link
  could not be detected there. The card's maximum was in sysfs the whole
  time — the backend just never resolved a PCI address, because that was
  derived from storcli output. It now walks there from the scsi_host, so
  both backends read the link, the card's ceiling and the slot's ceiling the
  same way. (#14)

###2026.08.06###
Everything below the Array Map is the same map you already built — placement
got easier, and it can now leave the machine. Thanks to @Waz and @jac2424,
whose sysfs output made the Host Link fix possible on hardware nobody here
owns.

**Fixed**
- Host Link no longer warns forever about a card in a slot narrower than
  itself. It compared the negotiated link against the CARD's maximum, so an
  x8 card the motherboard runs at x4 — normal on OEM servers and not
  fixable — reported a permanent fault, while a chipset-limited x4 was told
  it was "running at its full x4 width" when the card could do x8. The
  slot's own ceiling is now read from its PCIe bridge and the link judged
  against whichever of the two is lower, so both read correctly with no
  configuration. A link below what it could actually reach still warns.
  (#13, #14)
- Host Link claimed a SAS2 card was at "its full x4 width" when nothing had
  told it what that card's maximum was. The lsiutil backend reports no
  maximum at all, so there was nothing to be full of. It now says the link
  and stops there, rather than dressing an unmeasured link as a checked one.
  (#14)
- Double-clicking a bay to empty it did nothing, from 2026.08.05. Picking a
  drive up redraws the whole grid, so the two clicks landed on different
  elements and the browser never delivered the double-click to the bay.
- The Locate STOP button needed two presses. Any click on the Array Map
  redrew the Locate buttons from the state at the last refresh, so a button
  could show "Locate" over a drive that was still blinking — and pressing it
  then started a locate that was already running instead of stopping it.
- The bay map, Locate and Reset Baseline could all silently fail to save
  when firmware flashing was turned off. They share Unraid's CSRF token,
  which was only being read when flashing was enabled.

**Added**
- Drag a drive into its bay on the Array Map, or drag it back to the
  unassigned list to empty it. Click-then-click still works and is still the
  way to do it on a touch screen.
- **Copy map** and **Restore map**. The layout is built by hand and nothing
  on the machine can regenerate it, so it can now be copied out as text and
  pasted back — into a note, another server, or the file itself.
- **Clear map**, which empties the grid in one go after telling you how many
  drives it is about to unplace, and **Undo**, which puts the map back after
  a Clear or a grid resize. One level deep, and consumed when used.
- Expected PCIe Width and Generation in Settings, for boards whose PCIe
  bridge reports nothing and where Host Link therefore cannot work the slot
  out for itself. Leave both at 0 unless Host Link is wrong about your link;
  they are corrections, not mutes, and a link below what you set still warns.

**Changed**
- Firmware/BIOS Update is its own page now, under Settings → User Utilities,
  instead of a tab on the Monitor. Once flashing is enabled, a red button at
  the bottom of the Settings page opens it — and that is the only way in; the
  Monitor no longer links to it at all. It is the one part of this plugin that
  writes to hardware, so getting to it means passing the warning that explains
  what it costs to get wrong. With flashing off, neither the button nor the
  menu entry exists. Nothing about how flashing works has changed.
- HBA Health lays its controller cards out the same way, for the same reason.
  A narrow window still gets a single column. On a frame too tight to fit two
  cards at full width, the gauge and the temperature meter stack inside each
  card rather than the cards dropping back to one column — the card gets a
  little taller and the page loses a whole card's height.
- The unassigned drive list on the Array Map is ordered the way Unraid's Main
  page lists disks — Parity, Disk 1, Disk 2, then pools, then anything the
  array does not use — instead of the order the controller happened to
  report them in.
- Every tab is one width now. The panel used to size itself to whichever tab
  was open, so it changed shape as you moved along the strip.
- Tables and health rows lost the dead space between them, and the bay cards
  are shorter: the Unraid slot and the port share a line, and the model and
  serial keep their own so neither gets truncated.
- Placing a drive no longer reloads the whole Array Map. The bay updates
  immediately and the save happens behind it.

###2026.08.05###
Two new tabs, plus the topology fix that 2026.08.04.1 did not actually
deliver on SAS2 cards. Thanks to @jac2424, who confirmed the SAS2 fixes on
his own 9207-8i, and to @TheIlluminate92, whose diagnosis and diagnostic
bundle located the expander problem below where it was reported.

**Added**
- An Array Map tab. Enclosure/slot and PHY numbers are the controller's own
  addressing, and knowing a drive sits on PHY 14 does not tell you which of
  24 physical bays to walk over to. The Array Map is a grid you shape to
  match your chassis and then assign each drive to the bay it actually
  occupies. Bays are coloured by health and show temperature, so a warning
  becomes a place rather than a row. Once built it can be locked, since the
  map is the one thing here that cannot be regenerated by re-reading the
  hardware: it is you, walking to the rack.
- Locate, on any Drives row and on any assigned bay. The drive's activity
  light starts blinking in a steady rhythm so the row becomes a tray you can
  find. It needs nothing from the backplane and drives no LED — the plugin
  reads the drive twice a second and the tray light flickers because the
  drive is busy, which is why it works where a proper locate LED does not.
  Press again to stop; it also stops itself after five minutes so a
  forgotten blink cannot hold a disk awake. It does keep that drive awake
  while it runs, which is inherent to the technique.

**Fixed**
- Topology still read "0 drives" on SAS2 cards. The fix in 2026.08.04.1 was
  written against a sysfs layout these cards do not have — the kernel places
  two further directory levels between a SAS host and its drives, and the
  plugin looked only as deep as the first. The test meant to catch this
  built its fixture in the same wrong shape, so it passed, and that is why
  the earlier fix shipped believing it worked. The fixture was rebuilt from
  a real card's sysfs before a line of code changed. (#14)
- Drives showed the wrong PHY number on those same cards. Where the PHY
  could not be read, the plugin quietly fell back to the SCSI target id and
  displayed it as a PHY — a wrong number rather than a missing one, on six
  of eight drives on the reporting hardware. (#14)
- Link Integrity never left "Not enough samples yet" on a box with an
  expander. The card's own PHYs and the PHYs on an expander behind it were
  read into one list under colliding numbers, so every poll compared a real
  counter against an unrelated one, read the difference as a decrease, and
  discarded the history as unreliable. Hours of samples were thrown away
  each poll and it looked like patience was the answer. Only the card's own
  PHYs are read now. (#12)
- Array Map bay assignments now record which expander a drive sits behind.
  An expander numbers its own PHYs from zero, exactly as the card does, so
  on a chassis with one two drives could claim the same bay — and whichever
  was read last took it, leaving the other missing from the grid and from
  the unassigned tray both. On the reporting hardware — two expanders on
  one card — that was seven of nineteen drives missing. Cards without an
  expander are unaffected and every existing assignment keeps its bay,
  confirmed unchanged on a direct-attached card. (#12)

**Changed**
- HBA Health and PHY Health could report different error rates for the same
  controller at the same moment, with neither saying why. They answer
  different questions: one measures the recent samples it holds, the other
  averages across everything since the counters were first seen, so a burst
  that ended days ago keeps showing in one and not the other. Both now state
  the window they cover, and the PHY tab shows a recent rate on its own line
  under the long-run one. Neither number changed.
- Firmware/BIOS Update puts the controller cards side by side instead of
  stacking them. Nothing in a card is as wide as the frame, so on a two-HBA
  box half the page was empty and the second card sat below the fold. A
  narrow window still gets a single column.

###2026.08.04.1###
Everything reported on issue #11, plus two things found while fixing it.
Thanks to @jac2424, whose screenshots of a SAS9207-8i showed a healthy card
reporting nonsense.

**Fixed**
- Topology read "0 drives" on SAS2 cards. Cards read through lsiutil never
  counted their drives at all, so the row judged a hard-coded zero and still
  called it "all drives present" on a card with eight disks attached. The
  count now comes from the drives the controller's own SAS host actually
  presents. A controller that cannot report a count says so, instead of
  claiming zero. (#11)
- HBA Health rows gave numbers with nothing to read them against. "Link
  Integrity 0/hr" never said 0 of what, and "Read Health ok" said nothing at
  all. Every row now prints the sentence behind its value — what was measured,
  over what window, and why it is judged the way it is. Values that are words
  are capitalised to match the rest of the plugin. (#11)
- Drive names never appeared on the PHY Health tab. The tab read its drive
  list through a 60-second cache that it was the only user of, so the cache
  had always expired by the next visit and every visit got an empty answer.
  This also affected the drive names on the top-offenders list.

**Added**
- A Device column on the Drives and PHY Health tabs. Enclosure/slot, bus/target
  and PHY index are the controller's own addressing and line up with nothing on
  Unraid's Main page, so a row could not be matched to a disk without comparing
  SAS addresses by eye. Both tabs now lead with the same /dev name Main and the
  SMART tab use. Rows that cannot be resolved with certainty show a dash rather
  than a guess. (#11)

**Changed**
- The Settings page no longer leaves holes between its cards. Sections sat in a
  grid whose rows were as tall as their tallest card, so every short section
  left empty space beside it. They now pack.

###2026.08.04###
Everything reported on issue #10, plus a Health tab fix found while
investigating it. Thanks to @PaliKinG3 and @jac2424, who ran diagnostics on
their own hardware and found several of these.

**Fixed**
- Mode was missing entirely on 9200-series cards. The Mode row only ever
  appeared on cards read through storcli, so a SAS9207-8i showed the next
  field where Mode should be and it looked like a 93xx-only feature. It is now
  read from the flashed firmware image name, which states the personality
  directly. Verified on a SAS9207-8i. (#10)
- Mode could say IR on a card flashed to IT. On cards read through storcli the
  personality was inferred from drive states, and an unconfigured drive reports
  "UGood" whether it sits on an IT-only HBA or on an IR card with no arrays —
  so an IT-flashed SAS9305-16i was labelled IR. Mode is now reported only when
  the drive states actually prove one, and the row is hidden otherwise. A
  hidden row is better than a confident wrong answer. (#10)
- Chip was blank on some controllers. The chip name came from a built-in list
  of five device IDs, and any card outside it — a SAS9305-16i among them —
  showed nothing at all. It now comes from storcli's own report, which covers
  every chipset. (#10)
- SATA drives were labelled SAS, and every drive showed "Grown Defects" — a
  metric only SAS drives have. Drive type was taken from the bus, and every
  drive behind a SAS HBA reports itself as SAS whatever it actually is. The
  SMART tab now asks the drive rather than the bus, shows a Type column, and
  the column is renamed Reallocated, which is true on both. (#10)
- Link Integrity could read "errors rising (0/hr)". A genuine sub-1/hr error
  rate was rounded down to zero, so the HBA Health tab warned about a rising
  counter while showing it as nothing. Rates below 1/hr are now shown to one
  decimal, and a non-zero rate never displays as zero.

**Changed**
- The diagnostic bundle now collects the firmware personality, the drive
  transport and the SAS device class. Diagnosing issue #10 needed all three
  and none were in the bundle, so they had to be requested by hand. A test now
  fails the build if the bundle stops collecting something the plugin reads.

**Known**
- Opening the SMART tab can spin up a sleeping SATA drive if it sits behind a
  SAS HBA. Whether to use smartctl's "do not wake a sleeping disk" guard is
  decided from the bus, and every drive behind a SAS HBA reports as SAS — a
  bus whose health reads genuinely do not touch the platters. A SATA drive on
  that path is therefore read without the guard. This is not new in this
  release and nothing here changed it. Fixing it means changing when a
  sleeping array disk gets woken, which needs measuring on real hardware
  first. If you see drives spin up when you open the tab, please say so on the
  tracker — that report is the measurement.
###2026.08.03###
- Fixed: 2026.08.02 could not be installed. Unraid rejected the plugin file
  with "XML file doesn't exist or xml parse error" before downloading
  anything. A shell comment inside one of the plg's INLINE blocks contained
  angle brackets, which an XML parser reads as an unclosed tag; INLINE content
  is XML text, so a bare "&lt;" or "&amp;" anywhere in it — even in a comment —
  breaks installation for everyone. The comment is reworded; nothing else
  changed.
- Added: the test suite now parses hbaviewer.plg and fails on a bare "&lt;" or
  "&amp;" inside any INLINE block, on a missing version/pkgURL/md5 entity, on a pkgURL
  whose path does not match the version, and on a missing changelog block. CI
  linted every .php and .sh but never the one file that decides whether the
  plugin installs at all.
###2026.08.02###
The largest release so far: a new HBA Health tab, PHY error baselines, an
export endpoint, diagnostic bundles, notifications, and fixes for every open
issue on the tracker.

**New**
- HBA Health tab: five independent indicators (thermal, link integrity,
  topology, host link, controller read) with a worst-of rollup and a reason
  string naming the offending PHY. Anything that cannot be measured reads grey
  rather than green — a collector that times out must never look healthy.
- PHY error baselines: press Set Baseline per controller and every counter is
  shown as a delta and an errors/hour rate instead of a number that has been
  accumulating since the driver loaded. Stored on /boot so it survives reboots;
  a reboot or driver reload invalidates it rather than showing a negative delta.
- Top offenders: above the PHY table, the PHYs with the highest error rate since
  the baseline, each named by the drive it serves. A PHY that cannot be matched
  to exactly one drive says so rather than guessing at a bay.
- Notifications (opt-in, off by default): one Unraid notification each time a
  controller's health status changes, never a repeat while it persists.
- Diagnostic bundle: one button collects the raw storcli/lsiutil output, the
  sysfs state and the plugin's parsed JSON into one archive for a bug report,
  anonymised by default with one length-preserving map for the whole bundle.
- Export / API: a read-only JSON snapshot of every controller, plus the same
  data in Prometheus text format, linked from Settings. Session-gated, so an
  unauthenticated scraper cannot poll it.
- HOWTO.md and ARCHITECTURE.md.

**Fixed**
- Drives tab empty on controllers that report no enclosure. storcli answers the
  enclosure form with "No drive found!" on those cards and addresses drives as
  /cN/sN instead; that form is now used as a fallback. (#5, #6)
- PCIe width and speed missing on storcli cards, now read from sysfs. (#5)
- PCIe generation reported one notch low on SAS2/lsiutil cards — the IOUnit
  Page 7 speed field is an enum, not a bitmask like the width field beside it,
  so a card negotiating 8 GT/s read as Gen2. Gen1 rendered as nothing at all. (#9)
- False "warning" temperature notifications: five fixed bands now separate how
  hot the card is from whether the badge should complain, and the PHY error
  floor no longer trips on a handful of counts. (#8)
- Plugin colours now follow the Unraid theme instead of hardcoded values that
  only worked on one of them. (#7)
- The enclosure summary no longer reports "8 slots, 0 drives" above a table of
  drives; those counts describe a synthesised enclosure the drives are not
  attached to.
- Event archive no longer mixes entry shapes when a system changes backend.
- Firmware/BIOS Step 3 is greyed out while the array is running. The server-side
  guard was always there; the UI now says so before you fill the form in.

**Changed**
- Every per-controller tab renders one card per HBA.
- Two-column Settings layout.
- Dashboard tile: per-controller model and temperature, status colour, and a
  "no sensor" reading instead of 0 °C on cards without one.
- Test fixtures are now real controller output from reporters wherever possible,
  with identifiers masked length-preservingly.
###2026.07.27###
- Fixed: the Performance tab showed no temperature on SAS2 (9200-series) cards.
  Temperatures were matched with a pattern that never fit the lsiutil backend's
  output, so those controllers graphed nothing while the Overview and dashboard
  tile showed the reading correctly. On multi-controller systems a card that
  failed to report could also shift the next card's temperature onto it. (#2)
- Fixed: firmware version misread on SAS2 cards. The version is packed as four
  hex bytes, so a card reporting 14000700 is running 20.00.07.00 — it was shown
  as 14.00.07.00 and then flagged with a false "pre-P20" warning. (#3)
- Fixed: Settings no longer tells SAS2-only systems to install storcli. The
  check keyed off the mpt3sas driver being present, but mpt3sas also drives SAS2
  hardware, so cards that need nothing extra were reported as SAS3. (#3)
- Dashboard tile: new HBA card icon that takes the controller's status colour,
  and the header now shows model and temperature per controller — so the tile
  still answers at a glance when it is minimised. Cards with no onboard sensor
  say "no sensor" instead of 0 °C.
###2026.07.23###
- UI: one consistent text color across the Monitor tabs, Settings and the
  dashboard tile, replacing the several shades of gray that were harder to read
  on some themes. Appearance only — no change to what's collected or shown.
###2026.07.10.2###
- NEW: Performance tab — real-time per-controller graphs (throughput, IOPS,
  %util, latency, PHY error-rate, temperature). Sampled ~2s in the browser from
  /proc/diskstats + sysfs (zero-dependency, no sampler daemon, no flash writes);
  history is in-browser only and resets on reload. Toggle in Settings (default on).
- NEW (advanced, opt-in, OFF by default): Firmware/BIOS Update tab. Enable it in
  Settings to detect the card, run a read-only per-controller verify, upload a
  model-correct firmware image (auto-detects storcli / sas2flash / sas3flash, or
  upload the tool), and flash ONE controller behind hard guardrails — array must
  be stopped, typed-FLASH confirmation, single-flight lock — with a live log and
  reboot prompt. Flash tools are proprietary and never bundled. Read the README
  safety section first: a wrong image can permanently brick the controller.
- UI refresh: refreshed Monitor / Settings / dashboard-tile styling (rounded
  cards, cleaner tabs, temperature ring) and a branded plugin icon. No change to
  what's shown, just how it looks.
###2026.07.10.1###
- Internal architecture refactor — no visible change, same output on every tab.
  The storcli/lsiutil backend choice, controller enumeration, and driver
  detection are now one shared module (scripts/lib.sh hba_each); the
  PHY/Drives/Events tabs pick their columns from an explicit "backend" field
  instead of inferring it from the data shape, so a new column can't misfire
- Event-log archive and the cached background read are now standalone,
  unit-tested modules (event_archive.php, cached_read.php)
- Removed the duplicated backend_storcli.sh and fixed a driver-string detection
  order that could differ between code paths
###2026.07.09.2###
- Fixed "Backend produced no output" on the Overview when a slow storcli scan
  exceeded the web timeout — the hardware read now runs as a detached background
  job (the only hardware reader) and the page polls a result file until ready,
  so a slow first read can never fail or be thrown away
###2026.07.09.1###
- Monitor page now opens instantly with a "Loading HBA information" banner; the
  hardware read runs in the background (no more page-load hang)
- Settings page shows the Access Method (storcli / lsiutil) with zero lag
- Open HBAviewer Monitor button shows a load-time confirmation popup
###2026.07.09###
- Settings moved to User Utilities menu (was System Settings)
- lsiutil Port field now hidden for non-SAS2 controllers
- Redesigned settings buttons: 'Save Settings First' + 'Open HBAviewer Monitor' (conditional)
- Added note about monitor load time for slow systems
- Fixed XML compatibility for Unraid parser
- Dashboard tile display with multi-controller support
- Hard drive icon throughout interface
</CHANGES>

<FILE Name="/boot/config/plugins/hbaviewer/hbaviewer.txz" Run="upgradepkg --install-new">
<URL>&pkgURL;</URL>
<MD5>&md5;</MD5>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
rm -rf /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/hbaviewer
tar -xJf /boot/config/plugins/hbaviewer/hbaviewer.txz -C /
chmod +x /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/hbaviewer/hbaviewer.x86_64
chmod +x /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/hbaviewer/scripts/*.sh
# Unraid's plugin manager resolves the Plugins-page icon against
# plugins/NAME/, where NAME is this plg's name attribute — "HBAviewer",
# capital H — not the lowercase install directory. So the icon has to exist
# there too. Deliberately created here rather than shipped in the repo:
# HBAviewer/ and hbaviewer/ collide on case-insensitive filesystems, so a
# maintainer on Windows or macOS would see a phantom deletion on every checkout.
mkdir -p /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/HBAviewer
cp -f /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/hbaviewer/icon.png /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/HBAviewer/hbaviewer.png
if [ ! -f /boot/config/plugins/hbaviewer/hbaviewer.cfg ]; then
  mkdir -p /boot/config/plugins/hbaviewer
  printf "HBA_PORT=1\nALERT_THRESHOLD=76\n" > /boot/config/plugins/hbaviewer/hbaviewer.cfg
fi
# The one directory the user drops flash files into: the sas2flash/sas3flash
# tool, the firmware image, the optional BIOS. Survives updates and reboots, and
# stays mounted when the array is stopped — which flashing requires, and which
# is why this cannot live under appdata. There is no upload form: a multipart
# POST to any .php behind Unraid's nginx never completes.
# tools/ is the pre-2026.08.09 name, still created and still read so nobody who
# already put a flasher there is stranded.
mkdir -p /boot/config/plugins/hbaviewer/flash
mkdir -p /boot/config/plugins/hbaviewer/tools
# Health-status notifications (Settings > HBAviewer > Notifications, OFF by
# default). The entry is installed unconditionally and the script exits
# immediately when the toggle is off, so enabling it needs no reinstall — and a
# disabled feature costs one config read every 10 minutes, not a hardware scan.
# /etc/cron.d is tmpfs and this block re-runs on every boot, which is how every
# Unraid plugin schedules work; update_cron folds it into root's crontab.
# printf, not a heredoc, and no ampersand-redirect: this INLINE block is XML
# character data, where a raw opening angle bracket or ampersand is ill-formed.
# (The comment above line 93 already contains one and Unraid's parser tolerates
# it, but don't add more.) Same reason the cfg above uses printf. Stderr goes to
# a second explicit /dev/null rather than the 2-ampersand-1 form.
printf '%s\n' \
  '# Generated by the HBAviewer plugin - removed on plugin uninstall.' \
  '*/10 * * * * /usr/bin/php /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/hbaviewer/scripts/notify_check.php >/dev/null 2>/dev/null' \
  > /etc/cron.d/hbaviewer
/usr/local/sbin/update_cron
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
removepkg hbaviewer
rm -rf /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/hbaviewer
rm -rf /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/HBAviewer
# Pair of the install block's cron entry — an orphaned job pointing at a deleted
# script would run (and fail) every 10 minutes forever. update_cron rebuilds
# root's crontab without it.
rm -f /etc/cron.d/hbaviewer
/usr/local/sbin/update_cron
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
